{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General bias: model is systematically too low versus realized prices during uptrends/rallies (typical underprediction ~0.5–2.5 EUR, occasionally larger). Apply a positive level shift when recent momentum is positive (e.g., last 5–10d slope > 0).",
    "Horizon effect: underprediction grows with horizon in rising regimes (days ~10–25 most pronounced). Use a horizon-increasing uplift (small at 1–5d, larger mid-horizon, then taper).",
    "Post-spike behavior: model often assumes stronger mean reversion after sharp moves and therefore undershoots the subsequent elevated plateau. After a >2 EUR jump in the last ~3–7 days, reduce mean-reversion strength and keep forecasts closer to the new level (add uplift for ~1–3 weeks).",
    "Late-horizon crash artifact: in some cases the model projects an exaggerated selloff in the last third of the horizon (e.g., forecasts drifting to low 60s while realized stays mid/high 60s). Add a floor/anchoring to recent 10–20d average and cap downward drift unless recent realized prices are already falling.",
    "Volatility damping: forecast path is too smooth and misses sustained elevated volatility; when recent realized volatility is high, widen forecast range upward (especially in bullish regimes) rather than reverting to the mean."
  ]
}
